  "response": "Verified all three against the fixture source before ruling on them — the evidence in the JSON is accurate (`registry.ts:6` says `Plans`, `sidebar.ts:6` says `Billing`, `billing.tsx` has `metadata.title = \"Plans\"` and an inline `<title>Subscription</title>`, and `docs/` contains only `billing.md`, `commands.md`, `teams.md` — no `setup.md`).\n\n## The three findings\n\n### 1. `route_metadata_drift` — crime_00006 — **warn**\n\n`/settings/billing` carries five labels: path `billing`, component `PricingPage`, `metadata.title = \"Plans\"`, `<title>Subscription</title>`, nav `Plans` / `Billing`.\n\nSplit the evidence — it isn't all equally real:\n\n- **Real, and user-visible.** The inline `<title>` wins the document head over `metadata.title`, so a user sees *Subscription* in the browser tab, *Billing* in the sidebar, and *Plans* in the registry — three names for one page in one session. That's a product defect, not a naming quibble.\n- **Noise — ignore these legs.** `component: PricingPage` vs `file: billing.tsx` vs `path: /settings/billing`. Component names legitimately describe what a page renders, and file names legitimately track the route segment. The detector counting these toward \"4 competing ways\" is what drags confidence down to 0.65, the lowest of the three.\n\nNot a block, because the finding as emitted is partly noise and a reviewer has to do that split by hand. Blocking a merge on a 0.65-confidence naming finding teaches people to bypass the gate.\n\n### 2. `duplicated_navigation_source` — crime_00005 — **warn**, and fix it in the same change as #1\n\nThis is not an independent finding — it's the concrete, objective half of #1. The nav-label disagreement appears verbatim as evidence in both (`nav label in src/nav/registry.ts: Plans` / `sidebar.ts: Billing`). Two findings, one fix: pick the canonical name for the destination, and have `registry.ts` and `sidebar.ts` derive from one list rather than each declaring their own.\n\nTreating these as two tickets is the mistake to avoid here. Fixing #2 alone leaves `<title>Subscription</title>` in place and the page still has two names.\n\n### 3. `docs_code_drift` — crime_00030 — **warn**, but just fix it now\n\n`docs/teams.md:5` links `./setup.md`, which does not exist. Confidence 0.9 and it's a binary fact, not a judgement — the highest-confidence of the three and a one-line fix (write the file, or drop the link).\n\nThe reason it's still a warn rather than a block: it's a pre-existing baseline condition in an unrelated doc. A gate that fails a billing PR over a stale link in `teams.md` is noise. But there's no reason to defer it either — it costs less to fix than to triage.\n\n## What I'd actually gate on\n\nNone of the three blocks as a **baseline** finding. All three have `churn: 0` and `recency: 0` — they're pre-existing, so a hard gate on them punishes whoever next touches the file rather than whoever introduced the drift. crimes agrees with that read: it ranks them 6th, 5th, and 30th of 42 by aggregate risk (`agent_risk` 0.38 / 0.38 / 0.24), well below the `contract_drift` and `direct_date` findings.\n\nThe gate that's worth having is diff-scoped:\n\n- **Block** a diff that adds a *third* nav source for a destination, or introduces a new label for `/settings/billing` without touching the other three. That's the failure mode the finding actually describes — an agent updating one source and leaving the rest stale.\n- **Block** a diff that adds a docs link to a file that doesn't exist, or deletes a file something links to. Broken relative links are objective enough for CI.\n- **Warn** on everything already in the baseline, and fix the `/settings/billing` cluster as one deliberate change.\n\nOne thing to check before you rename anything: `/account/subscription` is flagged as an orphaned destination (crime_00034), and *subscription* is exactly the word in the billing page's `<title>`. That route may be a half-finished duplicate of this one — worth resolving which page owns the journey before you settle on the canonical label, or you'll pick a name and then have to pick again.",
